 "lp": -0.5529397130012512, "top": [["force", -0.5529397130012512], ["M", -2.150052309036255]]}, {"t": " MCS", "lp": -0.07297178357839584, "top": [[" MCS", -0.07297178357839584], [" everything", -2.9280495643615723]]}, {"t": "-only", "lp": -0.22606612741947174, "top": [["-only", -0.22606612741947174], [",", -2.190253734588623]]}, {"t": ").", "lp": -0.21278370916843414, "top": [[").", -0.21278370916843414], [" compute", -2.207744598388672]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.028079485520720482, "top": [["\n\n", -0.028079485520720482], [" \n\n", -3.9586901664733887]]}, {"t": "---", "lp": -1.0906630754470825, "top": [["---", -1.0906630754470825], ["For", -1.371464729309082]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute engines. This is set via the `xe` kernel module parameter (e.g., `modprobe xe ccs_mode=1`).\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 0 (default)**\n- CCS contexts are distributed across **all** CCS engines in a round-robin fashion.\n- This is the standard, balanced approach.\n\n**ccs_mode 1**\n- CCS contexts are pinned to a **single CCS engine** (typically the first one).\n- All compute work goes to one engine rather than being spread out.\n\n**ccs_mode 2**\n- CCS contexts are distributed across CCS engines but with a **different affinity/assignment strategy** than mode 0.\n- Historically this was used to work around scheduling quirks on certain hardware revisions.\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\n**ccs_mode 4** is used to **disable CCS entirely** and force all compute onto the **Main Compute Slice (MCS)**.\n\nYou'd reach for mode 4 when:\n\n- **You're hitting bugs or instability** tied to the dedicated CCS engines (some early Arc firmware/driver combos had issues).\n- **Debugging compute problems** where isolating to the MCS simplifies the picture.\n- **Specific workloads** that don't benefit from (or conflict with) the CCS partitioning.\n\n## Practical Note\n\nThe exact semantics of these modes have shifted across driver versions, and documentation has been sparse/inconsistent. If you're on a recent driver, the differences between modes 0, 1, and 2 are often subtle, and **mode 4 is the one with a clear, stable meaning** (force MCS-only).\n\n---"}